# SPLIT(1)

## NAME

split - split a file into pieces

## SYNOPSIS

| **split** [ **-**\&*n* ] [ file [ name ] ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Split* reads *file* and writes it in *n*-line pieces (default 1000), as many as necessary,
onto a set of output files. The name of the first output file is *name* with **aa** appended,
and so on lexicographically. If no output name is given, **x** is default.

If no input file is given, or if **-** is given in its stead, then the standard input file is
used.

**Note:** the pieces are created in the **working directory,** the name being handed to
fopen(3) with no directory part prepended. A *name* containing a **/** therefore writes
wherever it points; nothing is created for it, and a missing directory is reported as **Cannot
create output**. An existing piece is **truncated** without warning. Both are v7's and its page
said neither.

## HOW MANY PIECES

The suffix is two letters, so **676** pieces can be named: **aa** through **zz**.

**Note:** the 677th is refused, with **split: more than 676 output files**. v7 carried on past
**zz** into **{a**, **{b** and beyond, with no complaint - so the promise above that the names
run lexicographically stopped being true exactly when a user had enough pieces to care. What
has already been written is left written; the input has been consumed.

**Note:** *name* may be at most **97** bytes, the buffer being 100 and the suffix and
terminator taking three. A longer one is refused with **split: output name too long** before
anything is created. v7 copied it in with no test at all.

## DIAGNOSTICS

**Note: -0** is refused with **split: line count must be positive**. v7 accepted it and then
looped forever, reading nothing and writing nothing: with a count of zero the per-piece loop
runs no iterations, so no input is read, no output is opened, and the program goes round again.

**Note:** an unrecognised option is refused with a usage line. v7 ignored it silently and split
the file into thousand-line pieces the user had not asked for.

Every diagnostic above exits 1, as does an input that cannot be opened.

## SEE ALSO

cat(1), tee(1), wc(1)
